            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Jarvis 0.14.0</h2>
<p><b>You can see Jarvis working.</b> A line now sits beside the mark at the top
of your day and says what Jarvis is doing right now — reading Slack, opening a
thread, thinking, planning your day, or how many new tasks it just found. It
appears only while something is actually happening and clears itself when the
work is done.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>It only says true things.</b> Every line is a step that is really
      running. Nothing is a progress animation playing on a timer, so a quiet
      catch-up says less and a busy one says more.</li>
  <li><b>Each step stays long enough to read.</b> Some of Jarvis's steps finish
      in a fraction of a second; they now wait their turn instead of flickering
      past.</li>
  <li><b>Jarvis looks like he is thinking</b> — the eyes glance away and work
      while a run is in flight, and come back to you when it ends. Move your
      pointer and he looks at you instead.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>A simpler day.</b> The task list no longer prints an estimated duration
beside every task, and Jarvis no longer spends part of its thinking guessing
one. Your day is the work that needs you and the meetings you actually have.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Notes you send Jarvis are marked as notes</b> in the sources row, with
      a real glyph rather than an unexplained dot.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
